      <h1>Self-Discharge Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Self-discharge testing measures how fast a battery <strong>loses charge
        while idle</strong> — tracking voltage or capacity over a rest period. A healthy lead-acid battery loses
        roughly <strong>a few percent per month</strong> at room temperature; an abnormally fast loss signals
        <strong>internal shorts, contamination or sulfation</strong>. It is the diagnostic behind shelf-life claims.
      </div>

      <h2>What it measures and how</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Method</strong></td><td>Rest the charged battery, track voltage/capacity over days-weeks</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Normal (lead-acid, ~25°C)</strong></td><td>A few % per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Accelerated screening</strong></td><td>Higher temperature shortens the test</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Fast failure</strong></td><td>Internal short, metallic contamination, sulfation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why does this test matter?</h2>
      <p>
        Self-discharge decides <strong>shelf life</strong> — how long a battery survives in storage before it
        sulfates or drains itself (see
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/battery-date-code/">date codes</a> and
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/battery-sulfation/">sulfation</a>). It also
        <strong>screens quality</strong>: in manufacturing, cells with abnormally high self-discharge are the ones
        hiding defects, which is why the test is a standard sorting gate after formation.
      </p>

      <h2>Interpretation</h2>
      <p>
        A battery that drains several percent per <em>week</em> instead of per month is failing, not resting. The
        reading separates three conditions: normal (slow, chemistry-limited), high (aging or partial shorts), and
        catastrophic (internal short — lithium packs in particular must be quarantined at this point).
      </p>
      <h2>The expected rates</h2>
      <p>
        The benchmark: a healthy flooded battery loses roughly <strong>5–15% per month</strong> at room
        temperature (higher in heat), AGM somewhat less, and lithium well under <strong>1–2% per month</strong>.
        A battery losing far more than its chemistry's norm — or one cell dropping faster than its neighbours —
        is the test's signal that an internal defect is draining it (see
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/self-discharge/">self-discharge physics</a>).
      </p>


      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Where I stand:</strong> Self-discharge is the quietest diagnostic in batteries — nothing visibly
        happens, which is exactly why the test matters: it catches the failures that declare themselves only after
        weeks on a shelf or a year in a drawer.</p>
        <p><strong>Why I think so:</strong> The test reads the battery's internal leaks of charge — the earliest signature of
        shorts and contamination. For manufacturers it sorts defects; for buyers it explains why shelf-fresh matters;
        for everyone it is the difference between a battery that rests and one that dies quietly.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

